          "title": "The Classic Error",
          "items": [
            "`let Some(x) = opt;` — refutable pattern in local binding",
            "That error is exactly why `if let` exists",
            "And why `let else` was added"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Guards Never Count",
          "text": "Exhaustiveness is checked per pattern, guards stripped off. A guarded arm contributes zero coverage no matter how total it looks."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Case Only",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "if let Some(x) = opt { use_it(x) }",
            "// sugar for match with one real arm",
            "// and a silent `_ => {}`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`if let .. else { .. }` handles the non-matching side",
            "Chains as `else if let` for a run of patterns",
            "Trade-off: you give up exhaustiveness checking",
            "Use it when one case genuinely is the point"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "while let & let else",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Drain Loops"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "while let Some(top) = stack.pop() {",
            "    visit(top);",
            "}",
            "// while let Some(i) = it.next() == `for`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Loops until the pattern stops matching",
            "The canonical way to drain a stack or queue",
            "`for` desugars to exactly this over an iterator"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bind Or Bail"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "let Some(x) = opt else { return; };",
            "let Ok(cfg) = load() else {",
            "    return Err(Error::NoConfig);",
            "}; // x and cfg live on below"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`let else` stable since Rust 1.65",
            "The `else` block must diverge — no falling through",
            "`return`, `break`, `continue`, or `panic!`",
            "Kills rightward drift: happy path stays unindented",
            "Perfect for guard clauses at the top of a function"
          ]
        }
      ]
